      "math_academy_id": "e-954",
      "problem": "Express $\\left(-\\infty, -1 \\right) \\cup [2, \\infty)$ as an inequality.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we graph the two intervals on a number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191664/assets/ebc7db3faf2d9eafb887e93e9cf3613853914bf02ef87b6f63b638494c55a2ac.png)\n\nThe interval $\\left(-\\infty, -1 \\right)$ can be expressed as the inequality $x < -1.$\n\nLikewise, the interval $[2, \\infty)$ can be expressed as the inequality $x \\geq 2.$\n\nSo, the union of these intervals represents the compound inequality\n\n$$\nx < -1 \\text{ or } x \\geq 2.\n$$",
